= Practice Questions — Part 3 (Q11 to Q15)

#mcq(11, [Which specific pathogenic microorganisms are the causative agents of the venereal diseases Gonorrhoea and Syphilis, respectively?], (
  [Treponema pallidum and Neisseria gonorrhoeae],
  [Neisseria gonorrhoeae and Treponema pallidum],
  [Chlamydia trachomatis and Trichomonas vaginalis],
  [Herpes Simplex Virus and Human Papillomavirus],
))
#solution(11, [Gonorrhoea is caused by the Gram-negative diplococcus bacterium *Neisseria gonorrhoeae*. Syphilis is caused by the spirochete bacterium *Treponema pallidum*. Both infections are bacterial, transmitted primarily via sexual intercourse, and respond to antibiotic therapies if initiated prior to tertiary complications.], answer: [B])

#mcq(12, [In clinical reproductive medicine, a married couple is formally diagnosed with infertility only when they are unable to conceive after a minimum cohabitation period of:], (
  [6 months of unprotected sexual intercourse],
  [1 to 2 years of regular, unprotected sexual intercourse],
  [3 years of coitus accompanied by barrier contraceptives],
  [5 years of intermittent cohabitation],
))
#solution(12, [Infertility is defined clinically and epidemiologically as the failure to achieve clinical pregnancy after *1 to 2 years (minimum 12 to 24 months)* of regular, unprotected sexual intercourse without the use of any contraceptive measures.], answer: [B])

#mcq(13, [During an Assisted Reproductive Technology (ART) procedure involving In Vitro Fertilization (IVF), what determines whether ZIFT or IUT is performed?], (
  [ZIFT is performed if sperm count is low; IUT is performed if ovum quality is poor],
  [ZIFT transfers embryos up to 8 blastomeres into the fallopian tube; IUT transfers embryos with more than 8 blastomeres into the uterus],
  [ZIFT transfers embryos into the uterus; IUT transfers unfertilized gametes into the fallopian tube],
  [ZIFT is an in vivo procedure; IUT is strictly an in vitro procedure],
))
#solution(13, [In Embryo Transfer (ET) following IVF, the developmental stage dictates the anatomical transfer locus: the zygote or early embryo with *up to 8 blastomeres* is transferred into the fallopian tube (*ZIFT — Zygote Intra-Fallopian Transfer*), whereas an embryo with *more than 8 blastomeres* is transferred directly into the uterine cavity (*IUT — Intra-Uterine Transfer*).], answer: [B])

#mcq(14, [Gamete Intra-Fallopian Transfer (GIFT) is the assisted reproductive technology specifically recommended for a female patient who:], (
  [Has bilateral blocked fallopian tubes and an atrophic non-receptive uterus],
  [Cannot produce functional ova, but possesses patent fallopian tubes and a suitable uterine environment for conception and development],
  [Produces normal ova but whose husband has complete azoospermia],
  [Suffers from severe cervical incompetence preventing natural delivery],
))
#solution(14, [GIFT (Gamete Intra-Fallopian Transfer) is indicated for a female who is anovulatory (cannot produce ova) but possesses patent fallopian tubes and a healthy uterine environment capable of supporting gestation. An ovum from a healthy donor is transferred into her fallopian tube along with sperm, allowing fertilization and cleavage to occur *in vivo naturally within her body*.], answer: [B])

#mcq(15, [Intra-Cytoplasmic Sperm Injection (ICSI) is an advanced micromanipulation technique primarily employed to treat infertility caused by:], (
  [Complete absence of fallopian tubes in the female partner],
  [Severe male factor infertility including severe oligospermia, asthenozoospermia, or inability of sperm to penetrate the zona pellucida],
  [Failure of blastocyst hatching due to uterine fibroids],
  [Immunological rejection of the embryo during the second trimester],
))
#solution(15, [ICSI (Intra-Cytoplasmic Sperm Injection) is a specialized in vitro micromanipulation procedure wherein a single spermatozoon is injected directly into the cytoplasm of an ovum using a microscopic micropipette. It is the premier clinical solution for severe male-factor infertility, such as extreme oligospermia, immotile sperms, or defective acrosomal enzyme release.], answer: [B])

